If $q(p(x))$ is evaluated at $x=-4,$ $-3,$ $-2,$ $-1,$ $0,$ $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ $4,$ what is the sum of the nine values obtained in this way?

Let \[f(x) =
\begin{cases}
2x + 9 &\text{if }x<-2, \\
5-2x&\text{if }x\ge -2.
\end{cases}
\]Find $f(-7).$

solution:
This is the sum of the series $a_1 + a_2 + \ldots + a_{10}$ with $a_1 = \frac{2}{3}$ and $r = \frac{2}{3}$.

Thus,  \begin{align*}
S &= \frac{a(1-r^{n})}{1-r}= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{1-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{10}}{1-\frac{2}{3}}\\
& = \frac{2}{3}\cdot\frac{1-\frac{1024}{59049}}{\frac{1}{3}}=\frac{2}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{1}\cdot\frac{58025}{59049}=\frac{2\cdot58025}{59049}\\
& = \boxed{\frac{116050}{59049}}.
\end{align*}

We can start by multiplying the first equation by 7 and the second equation by 4 to get rid of the coefficients of $y$:
\begin{align*}
84x + 147y &= 105 \\
84x + 48y &= 204
\end{align*}
Now we can subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate $x$:
\begin{align*}
(84x + 147y) - (84x + 48y) &= 105 - 204 \\
99y &= -99 \\
y &= -1
\end{align*}
Substituting this value of $y$ into either of the original equations, we can solve for $x$:
\begin{align*}
12x + 21(-1) &= 15 \\
12x - 21 &= 15 \\
12x &= 36 \\
x &= 3
\end{align*}
Thus, the ordered pair $(x,y)$ is $\boxed{(3,-1)}$.
The answer is (3,-1)

